WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:06.166
This is Anthropic, Read Aloud. Today: Investing in the public benefit.

2
00:00:07.466 --> 00:00:09.360
Investing in the public benefit.

3
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:14.396
From Anthropic Newsroom, published October 18, 2026.

4
00:00:15.396 --> 00:00:25.332
Today, we’re committing a portion of Anthropic’s shares to the Anthropic Public Benefit Fund, a new entity with a structure that increases the stake as the company grows.

5
00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:39.010
Subject to stockholder approval, 5 percent of Anthropic’s shares will be committed today, with an additional 2 percent added at the board’s discretion for every 50 percent increase in the company’s value above a set baseline.

6
00:00:39.460 --> 00:01:02.353
As a public benefit corporation, most of our work in pursuit of our mission takes place inside the company: research relevant to A I safety and interpretability; applied biomedical research; discounts on Claude for nonprofits and scientific institutions. These activities have both a public benefit component and for-profit value, so we house them directly in the company.

7
00:01:02.803 --> 00:01:18.184
The Anthropic Public Benefit Fund will have increased flexibility and scale, enabling us to pursue our mission in new ways: for example, we might explore large donations to traditional nonprofits or direct distributions to specific populations.

8
00:01:18.634 --> 00:01:40.257
One example is labor market impacts. A I may generate enormous economic value, but it could also cause instability for many workers. Anthropic has already committed 200 dollarsmillion to studying and addressing this issue, but given the pace of A I progress, bolder approaches may be needed. The Fund will have the versatility to pursue them.

9
00:01:40.707 --> 00:02:00.474
We’re establishing the Anthropic Public Benefit Fund as a wholly-owned LLC, rather than a 501(c)(3) or other format, to give adjustability between different forms of giving. None of the Anthropic equity held by the LLC will be used for lobbying or political giving, in the U S or abroad.

10
00:02:00.924 --> 00:02:19.415
Because of the way the Fund is structured, the more Anthropic grows, the larger the share of the company that can be dedicated expressly to the public good. It is common practice for tech companies to offer escalating compensation awards to their executives. We are instead directing that equity to the public benefit.

11
00:02:19.865 --> 00:02:41.914
This builds on commitments our co-founders and employees have already made. When Anthropic was founded, our co-founders pledged to donate 80 percent of their equity holdings, and since founding we have also operated an equity donation match program that allows employees to pledge equity which Anthropic matches. Approximately half of our employees participate in this program.

12
00:02:42.034 --> 00:02:55.209
When combined with today’s announcement, roughly 20 percent of Anthropic shares will be dedicated to the public good, plus any additional percentage that could be added if the company reaches specific valuation milestones.

13
00:02:55.659 --> 00:03:11.738
In the coming months, we will share more about the Anthropic Public Benefit Fund’s structure and governance, including the valuation milestones that may add to its stake. We will also report on our progress to date against our mission in an upcoming public benefit report.

14
00:03:12.738 --> 00:03:29.451
That was Investing in the public benefit, from Anthropic Newsroom, published October 18, 2026. Read the original at anthropic dot com. This unofficial audio edition was generated automatically for the podcast Anthropic, Read Aloud.
